  "subject": "🛢️ Oil pain persists",
  "date": "Wed, 21 Oct 2026 11:58:30 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Plus: French fears | Wednesday, October 21, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Macro \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Neil Irwin and Courtenay Brown &middot; Oct 21, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n 🛢️ The oil market's hopes for relief next year are fading fast, potentially complicating the macroeconomic backdrop for central bankers. More below.\n\n Plus, France's unrest in the streets continues to spill over into the bond market. Today's newsletter, edited by Jeffrey Cane and copy edited by Katie Lewis, is 764 words, a 3-minute read. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Oil&#x27;s long shadow \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Data: FactSet; Chart: Courtenay Brown/Axios Traders are betting that oil prices will remain elevated for longer than they anticipated just weeks ago, threatening to prolong the inflationary pressures that businesses and consumers have weathered for months.\n\n Why it matters: Oil inventories have helped cushion the blow from disrupted Middle Eastern supplies, but much of that buffer has been depleted since the war began.\n\n The possibility of sustained price pressures into next year throws into doubt the logic that central banks traditionally rely upon to look through energy price shifts — that they represent a one-time shift in relative prices, not an ongoing inflationary process. That leaves the global economy more vulnerable to another energy price surge, with potentially more lasting consequences for growth, inflation and interest rates than energy shocks of yore. By the numbers: Oil futures for delivery in 2027 — contracts to buy or sell crude next year — have climbed to their highest levels since the Iran war began.\n\n Prices for January 2027 are up 17% since early September, to more than $97 a barrel. Markets still anticipate some relief next year, but considerably less than they expected just weeks ago. It's a reversal from late summer, when hopes of easing tensions with Iran and a recovery in Middle Eastern oil supplies briefly pushed futures lower. June futures have jumped 18%, to $88 a barrel, while December futures are up 13%, to about $81. State of play: Crude oil exports from the Persian Gulf, including shipments through the Strait of Hormuz and alternative routes, surpassed prewar levels in the week ending Oct. 18, according to the Wall Street Journal . But the costly workarounds enabling that recovery have their limits.\n\n Those include ship-to-ship transfers outside the Strait of Hormuz, where security risks have deterred some vessels. \"You're coming up against limitations on actually how much volume you can move out of the Middle East, even with these processes,\" Christopher Haines, global head of oil at Energy Aspects, tells Axios. Restoring confidence among shipping companies and insurers that vessels won't be attacked will be \"exceptionally hard,\" Haines adds. Zoom in: Economist Robin Brooks argues that oil prices reflect higher shipping costs and fears of further attacks more than actual shortages.\n\n Using earlier monthly data, he estimates that Persian Gulf crude exports had recovered to 91% of prewar levels, with a \"temporary fear factor\" adding at least $20 a barrel. What to watch: The Trump administration is seeking to ease fuel prices ahead of the midterms, most recently by allowing more Russian diesel onto the market.\n\n But relief may be minimal: Ukrainian attacks have damaged Russian refineries, while sanctions complicate repairs, limiting additional exports, Haines says. The bottom line: \"The market narrative has shifted from \"transitory\" to \"new normal\" right before our eyes,\" Bob Elliot, chief investment officer at Unlimited Funds, wrote this morning . \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n Make sharper media decisions in 2027 \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Media Trends Executive annual membership includes the Annual Report plus year-round reporting, analysis and original data on media's biggest shifts.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n🔒 Get the report by becoming a Media Trends Executive member.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. New multidecade high for French yields \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Data: FactSet; Chart: Courtenay Brown/Axios French borrowing costs are heading unrelentingly upward, making for a slow-moving fiscal debacle in Europe's second-largest economy.\n\n Why it matters: It shows the risks for other advanced countries, including the U.S., as they face the combined pressure of large deficits, political resistance to austerity and a wary bond market.\n\n Driving the news: The benchmark 10-year French government bond yielded 5.01% as of 11:35am ET — a new post-2002 high.\n\n Also this morning, the spread between French yields and comparable German bunds widened to about 1.6 percentage point, the biggest spread in 15 years, per Bloomberg . Given that the two countries share a currency, central bank and a deeply intertwined economy, it is a sign of bond market revolt against French fiscal policy and worry about its political future. State of play: Riots and looting over the weekend were the latest evidence that any effort to restrain French fiscal deficits could come with extreme political blowback.\n\n The events enhanced the market-priced odds of the right-wing National Rally party prevailing in next spring's elections. The party has promised a slew of populist, deficit-busting policies like lowering energy taxes and reversing pension reforms. Traders on Polymarket now put the odds at nearly a coin flip on whether the National Rally's leader, Marine Le Pen, will be the next president of France: 46% as of this morning, up from 5.5% as recently as June. The bottom line: Political risk is back in the eurozone, with France on the front lines.\n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n Simplify how you manage \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Simplify for Management is a bootcamp on time, talent, feedback, and communication, built specifically for people managers.\n\n Managers work through it together and leave with their own custom AI tool built for how they lead.\n\n Learn more about Simplify for Management \n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
